WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:05.550
Inner Mongolia is China’s Montana.

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:12.290
Its winters are cold, its summers, hot, and
its wide open spaces isolated and unforgiving.

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:18.970
…Which makes it a very strange place to
build a giant 200-foot-tall red Victorian

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:20.140
palace.

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:26.349
Sure, the rich and famous have been known
to make some… unorthodox architectural decisions.

00:00:26.349 --> 00:00:32.860
But way out here near the Gobi desert, you
won’t find any tennis courts, golf clubs,

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:34.590
or art museums…

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:37.600
nevermind a Trader Joe’s.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:44.469
In fact, the only amenities include a highway
interchange to nowhere, a non-functional airstrip,

00:00:44.469 --> 00:00:49.250
and an Indiana Jones-style cardboard-cutout
city.

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:52.410
This place was never meant to be lived in.

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:57.820
It was built as target practice, on China’s
largest military base.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:05.030
And the People’s Liberation Army clearly
had a very specific mission in mind.

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:10.101
This is that 200-foot-tall palace in the desert.

00:01:10.101 --> 00:01:15.850
And this is Taiwan’s Presidential Office
Building in downtown Taipei.

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:20.800
Lest you mistake the replica for a mere roadside
attraction, state media released footage of

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:25.720
Chinese troops using it to simulate an invasion.

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:28.810
Needless to say, this is deeply unsettling.

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:34.579
That the Chinese military is actively practicing
an invasion of its small, democratic neighbor

00:01:34.579 --> 00:01:37.690
should attract the world’s attention and
outrage.

00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:38.820
…And…

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:43.720
it’s not evidence of an imminent plan to
attack.

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:48.040
We know that because it was built nine years
ago.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:52.790
Yet the failure of our worst fears to materialize
hasn’t prevented the same dire warnings

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:57.299
from being recycled year after year since.

00:01:57.299 --> 00:02:02.350
Just a few months ago, for example, U.S. Air
Force General Mike Minihan wrote a memo to

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:06.320
his 50,000 subordinates that began with “I
hope I am wrong.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:10.509
My gut tells me we will fight in 2025”.

00:02:10.509 --> 00:02:14.620
Another ‘Mike’ from the U.S. military
– Admiral Mike Gilday – made headlines

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:21.020
last year for saying he “couldn’t rule
out” a Chinese attack in 2022 or 3.

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:24.860
Each time, the evidence simply accumulates.

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:30.150
Every authoritative prediction or alarmist
headline reinforces the perception that war

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:32.430
is inevitable or even imminent.

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:37.019
“Where there’s smoke, there must be fire.”

00:02:37.019 --> 00:02:41.709
But Taiwan has now been “on the brink of
war” for most of a decade.

00:02:41.709 --> 00:02:47.150
Which means we’re long overdue for some
reflection.

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:53.240
Sponsored by “China, Actually”, my 6-part
series available only on Nebula.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:56.459
Let’s start with what we know…

00:02:56.459 --> 00:03:02.599
First, few countries have transformed so completely
as China since 1949.

00:03:02.599 --> 00:03:08.930
Yet, despite these 74 years of continuous
tumultuous change – during which not even

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:15.900
the written language survived untouched – on
one issue China has been remarkably consistent.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:21.790
Every leader since Mao has reaffirmed the
importance of unification.

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:26.290
Despite having never governed Taiwan, the
Party defines itself as the rightful heir

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:33.640
to and guardian of Chinese history, culture,
and, more problematically, the Han ethnicity.

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:38.980
America’s involvement in the region and
Taiwan’s growing national identity certainly

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:43.989
add insult to injury, but the mere existence
of another government that claims the label

00:03:43.989 --> 00:03:47.959
“Chinese” is already perceived as a threat.

00:03:47.959 --> 00:03:54.590
Second, we know that in the last two decades,
China has rapidly modernized its military.

00:03:54.590 --> 00:04:01.079
Truthfully, neither the size of its fleet
nor its budget are perfect measures of anything.

00:04:01.079 --> 00:04:05.569
Chinese ships are generally smaller and its
budget, easily distorted.

00:04:05.569 --> 00:04:10.459
Besides, how does one accurately discount
the abilities of an army that hasn’t fought

00:04:10.459 --> 00:04:14.480
a single major war for nearly half a century?

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:19.430
But although experts disagree about the relative
strength of the Chinese and American militaries,

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:23.660
there’s no doubt the gap between them is
shrinking.

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:28.460
What was once a guaranteed U.S. victory is
now uncertain.

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:34.710
Finally, we also, of course, know that – despite
its explicit intent and growing capability

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:38.320
– China hasn’t yet invaded.

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:44.110
The question, and where facts become speculation,
is why not?

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:50.539
One interpretation is that China is waiting
to cross some threshold of tactical superiority.

00:04:50.539 --> 00:04:56.320
Once it does – which could be any day now
– war is inevitable.

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:59.940
The advantage of this theory is its simplicity.

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:05.720
When Xi Jinping says Taiwan, quote, “must
and will be reunited with China”, it doesn’t

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:10.660
take a wild imagination to hear “we can
and will invade”.

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:15.460
When he repeatedly mentions the year 2049
as the target for China’s “rejuvenation”,

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:21.180
it’s not outlandish to interpret this as
a hard deadline for unification.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:25.880
After all, it isn’t as if this increasing
assertiveness is confined to words.

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:32.280
Here’s a timeline of recent Chinese “gray
zone” tactics aimed at Taiwan – coercive

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:37.759
activities that fall somewhere between normal
diplomacy and outright war.

00:05:37.759 --> 00:05:41.509
For instance, in 2016 it suspended all communication.

00:05:41.509 --> 00:05:48.490
In 2019, its fighter jets flew closer to the
island than they had in the previous two decades.

00:05:48.490 --> 00:05:54.289
And the next year, Beijing stopped importing
Taiwanese pineapples, one of its largest industries.

00:05:54.289 --> 00:06:00.240
Clearly, proponents of this theory aren’t
hallucinating – tensions are undeniably

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:02.500
growing.

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:07.130
But although intuitive, this theory is not
without its weaknesses.

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:13.850
Namely, if war is inevitable, why give your
opponent a specific timeline?

00:06:13.850 --> 00:06:18.110
Generally speaking, politicians prefer to
keep their options open.

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:21.230
Why needlessly impose on yourself a deadline?

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:27.380
At a time when the U.S. and Taiwan are looking
for reasons to increase their defense budgets,

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:31.940
why give them the literal and rhetorical ammunition
they need?

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:37.150
China’s best hope for success would involve
quick, decisive action, giving America little

00:06:37.150 --> 00:06:38.770
time to respond.

00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:44.900
Yet, not only does China not hide its intentions,
it releases footage of its replica presidential

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:46.620
building online!

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:51.960
If that first theory sees the current moment
as a critical turning point where China is

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:56.960
on the verge of finally following through
on its promises, a second one sees as much

00:06:56.960 --> 00:06:59.910
continuity as it does change.

00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:04.319
Sure, China has recently become more aggressive.

00:07:04.319 --> 00:07:10.129
But this timeline only goes back to 2016,
during which Taiwan has only had one president

00:07:10.129 --> 00:07:12.440
and one ruling party.

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:17.660
Now watch what happens when we extend the
timeline to the year 2000.

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:23.150
From then until 2008, Taiwan was led by the
“green” Democratic Progressive Party,

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:26.569
which is skeptical of engagement with China.

00:07:26.569 --> 00:07:31.729
The DPP, at that time, inched closer to declaring
independence than it ever has.

00:07:31.729 --> 00:07:37.039
…Close enough that, after China protested,
George Bush stepped in to warn the island

00:07:37.039 --> 00:07:39.680
not to proceed.

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:44.430
Following this incident, Beijing enacted an
“Anti-Secession Law” in 2005, which made

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:50.530
explicit its longstanding threat to invade
if Taiwan declared independence.

00:07:50.530 --> 00:07:57.181
All this changed in 2008, when the DPP lost
to the “blue” Kuomintang, or KMT, which

00:07:57.181 --> 00:08:02.330
promotes engagement with China and is strongly
opposed to independence.

00:08:02.330 --> 00:08:07.470
For the next eight years, its relations with
China were the warmest they’ve ever been.

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:11.820
The two presidents met, shook hands, and signed
a free trade agreement.

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:17.460
Today, it may feel like a lifetime away, but
less than a decade ago, many laughed at the

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:21.539
idea that China would invade an economy it
was investing billions of dollars in.

00:08:21.539 --> 00:08:28.220
Then, in 2016, the DPP returned to power.

00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:32.599
While it no longer advocates independence,
it has strengthened its unofficial relationship

00:08:32.599 --> 00:08:34.130
with the United States.

00:08:34.130 --> 00:08:40.180
In response, China cut off all communication,
banned many Taiwanese imports, and conducted

00:08:40.180 --> 00:08:43.019
increasingly elaborate military exercises.

00:08:43.019 --> 00:08:48.709
Now, it’s impossible to prove whether China
would’ve become more hostile even if the

00:08:48.709 --> 00:08:54.260
KMT had remained in power, but this timeline
certainly suggests a clear relationship between

00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:57.830
the Taiwanese government and China’s approach.

00:08:57.830 --> 00:09:03.660
This is not at all to blame Taiwan – Beijing
chooses its reactions.

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:08.760
It does, however, suggest a logic to this
reprehensible aggression.

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:12.200
Think of China’s position as a spectrum.

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:16.620
On one side is its ideal outcome – unification.

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:19.510
On the other side are its “red lines”.

00:09:19.510 --> 00:09:24.760
Most believe that China would invade today
if Taiwan declared independence.

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:30.210
In between is a range of more and less tolerable
scenarios.

00:09:30.210 --> 00:09:34.320
With the KMT in charge, independence is off
the table.

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:40.240
China relaxes, and uses carrots to gently
pull Taiwan toward unification.

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:45.800
When the DPP is elected, China switches to
a coercive “stick” approach to push Taiwan

00:09:45.800 --> 00:09:48.839
away from its red lines.

00:09:48.839 --> 00:09:55.060
When viewed this way, Xi’s speeches sound
a lot more like threats than announcements.

00:09:55.060 --> 00:09:59.529
When he seems to contradict himself by saying,
quote, “We will continue to strive for peaceful

00:09:59.529 --> 00:10:00.529
reunification…

00:10:00.529 --> 00:10:05.100
but we will never promise to renounce the
use of force.”, you could stress the “force”

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:09.560
part and dismiss the word “peaceful” as
nakedly disingenuous.

00:10:09.560 --> 00:10:14.370
After all, China has been disingenuous about
Hong Kong and Xinjiang and the South China

00:10:14.370 --> 00:10:15.870
Sea.

00:10:15.870 --> 00:10:20.610
You could also do the opposite and ignore
what’s obviously a threat.

00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:26.930
But there’s a third possibility: these two
promises aren’t contradictory.

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:30.339
China genuinely means both.

00:10:30.339 --> 00:10:36.450
On one hand, the idea of promising not to
use force is seen as a dangerous concession.

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:40.520
Because it’s the only thing preventing Taiwan
from declaring independence, it would forever

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:43.610
close the door to unification.

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:49.940
At the same time, waging an actual war would
be extraordinarily costly.

00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:55.570
Taiwan produces roughly 92% of the world’s
most advanced semiconductors.

00:10:55.570 --> 00:11:00.529
Nearly a fifth of the Chinese population lives
in the three provinces closest to Taiwan.

00:11:00.529 --> 00:11:06.660
And 90% of the world’s largest ships transit
through the Taiwan Strait.

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:10.890
Even if they could take the longer route through
Indonesia, it would increase shipping costs

00:11:10.890 --> 00:11:14.570
by an estimated half a billion dollars each
month.

00:11:14.570 --> 00:11:23.000
Collectively, a one-year war would reduce
America’s GDP by 5-10% and China’s by

00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:26.120
25-35%, according to one study from 2016.

00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:31.540
Now, let’s be clear: these are wild guesses.

00:11:31.540 --> 00:11:36.459
No one can realistically predict the outcome
of a war between two nuclear powers with any

00:11:36.459 --> 00:11:38.190
accuracy.

00:11:38.190 --> 00:11:42.380
The only thing we can say with certainty is
that such a conflict would fall somewhere

00:11:42.380 --> 00:11:47.510
between absolutely catastrophic and existential.

00:11:47.510 --> 00:11:52.829
Xi Jinping’s 2049 milestone for “China’s
national rejuvenation” includes economic

00:11:52.829 --> 00:11:58.899
prosperity – something incompatible with
any invasion of Taiwan – whether successful

00:11:58.899 --> 00:12:00.220
or not.

00:12:00.220 --> 00:12:05.720
So, yes, China does prefer peaceful unification.

00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:11.200
Of course it would rather achieve all of its
goals without suffering any of the costs.

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:16.470
Similarly, projecting a willingness to fight
offers many of the benefits of actually fighting

00:12:16.470 --> 00:12:18.790
but few of the costs.

00:12:18.790 --> 00:12:22.900
‘Toughness’ plays well to a domestic audience.

00:12:22.900 --> 00:12:26.700
It appeases a powerful political faction – the
military.

00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:29.670
It helps build Xi’s personal image.

00:12:29.670 --> 00:12:34.450
It’s an insurance policy against embarrassment
in case of a confrontation.

00:12:34.450 --> 00:12:38.089
…And it deters independence!

00:12:38.089 --> 00:12:41.330
2049 could be a genuine deadline.

00:12:41.330 --> 00:12:47.449
It could also be, as the 100th anniversary
of the PRC, a symbolic placeholder that feels

00:12:47.449 --> 00:12:54.850
far away, yet signals conviction, yet is also
vague and non-binding – the perfect political

00:12:54.850 --> 00:12:56.540
‘promise’.

00:12:56.540 --> 00:13:01.740
Some observers claim Xi has urged the PLA
to, quote, “date to fight”.

00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:07.329
But other Chinese speakers argue the phrase
could just as easily mean “dare to struggle”.

00:13:07.329 --> 00:13:11.750
At the very least, his choice of words is
deliberately ambiguous.

00:13:11.750 --> 00:13:19.220
By definition, deterrence only works when
indistinguishable from actual mobilization.

00:13:19.220 --> 00:13:23.930
In other words, it’s impossible to prove
whether China is simply deterring independence

00:13:23.930 --> 00:13:27.910
or genuinely preparing for an attack.

00:13:27.910 --> 00:13:33.720
Having a larger, stronger military is useful
whether or not it fights.

00:13:33.720 --> 00:13:38.560
When taken as a whole, all the evidence proves
definitively is that we simply don’t know

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:43.180
when or if China will invade.

00:13:43.180 --> 00:13:49.199
The trouble with this indeterminate, conflicting
“evidence” is that it’s ripe for cherry-picking.

00:13:49.199 --> 00:13:53.339
Sometimes this ambiguity is cynically exploited.

00:13:53.339 --> 00:13:59.910
The more urgent the threat facing America,
the more resources are directed to the military.

00:13:59.910 --> 00:14:04.990
But there are legitimate, or at least, understandable,
reasons, too.

00:14:04.990 --> 00:14:11.389
The military’s fundamental, and in fact,
singular mission is to prepare for every contingency,

00:14:11.389 --> 00:14:14.100
no matter how unlikely.

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:21.000
The real scandal would be if Admiralty Mike
Gilday did rule out a Chinese attack in 2023.

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:27.070
Or if General Mike Minihan wasn’t anticipating
the worst possible outcome.

00:14:27.070 --> 00:14:31.529
Anyone who reads General Minihan’s memo
in full will see that it clearly wasn’t

00:14:31.529 --> 00:14:37.509
written for a civilian audience – it uses
Air Force jargon and adopts the tone of a

00:14:37.509 --> 00:14:40.430
stern, confident superior.

00:14:40.430 --> 00:14:45.350
No one has to be doing their job poorly for
the threat to be exaggerated.

00:14:45.350 --> 00:14:49.769
The issue may be that someone is doing their
job.

00:14:49.769 --> 00:14:54.930
The problem arises when this mindset escapes
the walls of the Pentagon.

00:14:54.930 --> 00:15:00.240
In the media, not only do these warnings lose
their institutional context – the organizational

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:05.470
paranoia that we, the public, demand from
the armed forces – but they carry undue

00:15:05.470 --> 00:15:07.329
credibility.

00:15:07.329 --> 00:15:11.199
At the end of the day, these are just guesses!

00:15:11.199 --> 00:15:16.240
That fact is confirmed by the sheer diversity
of predictions.

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:19.850
Despite this, they carry significant authority.

00:15:19.850 --> 00:15:24.690
As one political correspondent wrote, “I
don’t know if or when China will invade

00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:25.690
Taiwan…

00:15:25.690 --> 00:15:28.940
But I figure that when someone like four-star
Air Force general Mike Minihan… writes…we

00:15:28.940 --> 00:15:33.209
should probably sit up and take notice.”

00:15:33.209 --> 00:15:36.470
The United States should prepare for an invasion.

00:15:36.470 --> 00:15:42.149
The consequences of a potential conflict would
be so devastating that we simply can’t afford

00:15:42.149 --> 00:15:45.269
not to take the threat seriously.

00:15:45.269 --> 00:15:50.570
After all, Putin’s war in Ukraine demonstrates
that no one’s sober, rational judgment can

00:15:50.570 --> 00:15:52.959
be taken for granted.

00:15:52.959 --> 00:15:58.410
The challenge is to prepare for war as if
it were inevitable, without succumbing to

00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:01.320
a self-fulfilling prophecy.

00:16:01.320 --> 00:16:07.350
To train for a sprint, while not losing sight
of the fact that we may be running a marathon.

00:16:07.350 --> 00:16:13.040
If conflict is a chronic, not acute challenge,
for example, America would be better off investing

00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:18.500
in, not diversifying away, from the Taiwanese
economy, which only makes it more dependent

00:16:18.500 --> 00:16:20.370
on China.

00:16:20.370 --> 00:16:26.120
U.S.-China tensions are unlikely to dramatically
improve in any lasting way in the foreseeable

00:16:26.120 --> 00:16:27.130
future.

00:16:27.130 --> 00:16:32.160
Which makes careful management of that relationship
more important than ever.

00:16:32.160 --> 00:16:36.779
But it’s precisely because an invasion of
Taiwan may not be guaranteed that we should

00:16:36.779 --> 00:16:42.260
take it seriously – our actions may influence
the outcome.

00:16:42.260 --> 00:16:48.709
If an invasion isn’t guaranteed, exaggerating
the threat could do more harm than good.

00:16:48.709 --> 00:16:55.459
As experts Ryan Hass, Bonnie Glaser, and Richard
Bush point out, while an actual war is hypothetical,

00:16:55.459 --> 00:17:00.660
China is already engaged in a psychological
war of attrition.

00:17:00.660 --> 00:17:05.510
Contrary to what many Americans assume, no
individual Chinese missile, fighter jet, or

00:17:05.510 --> 00:17:09.040
speech provokes much fear in Taiwan.

00:17:09.040 --> 00:17:14.509
But cumulatively, China’s objective is to
slowly wear down Taiwan’s will to resist

00:17:14.509 --> 00:17:20.079
– to propagate a general sense of uncertainty
and insecurity.

00:17:20.079 --> 00:17:26.179
In a 2017 survey, just 41% of the Taiwanese
public believed China would attack if Taiwan

00:17:26.179 --> 00:17:28.670
declared independence.

00:17:28.670 --> 00:17:33.370
Three years later, that number rose to 61%.

00:17:33.370 --> 00:17:38.810
If America’s pessimism is more fatalist
than motivational, it risks doing China’s

00:17:38.810 --> 00:17:40.180
work for it.

00:17:40.180 --> 00:17:46.250
And finally, if or when we do detect signs
of an imminent attack, one of America’s

00:17:46.250 --> 00:17:50.059
first actions will be to notify its allies.

00:17:50.059 --> 00:17:55.520
Crying ‘wolf’ today would undermine its
credibility tomorrow.

00:17:55.520 --> 00:18:00.000
In the early 2000s, for example, a Chinese
general made headlines for claiming “hundreds”

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:06.220
of U.S. cities would be targeted with nuclear
weapons if America defended Taiwan in a war.

00:18:06.220 --> 00:18:11.200
This comment, and others like it, led to panicked
speculation about the true size of China’s

00:18:11.200 --> 00:18:13.470
nuclear stockpile.

00:18:13.470 --> 00:18:15.620
But this theory is likely wrong.

00:18:15.620 --> 00:18:19.470
And this kind of exaggeration does no good
for Taiwan.

00:18:19.470 --> 00:18:26.059
I explain why in this episode of “China,
Actually”, which is available only on Nebula.

00:18:26.059 --> 00:18:31.270
In that same series, we also explore four
other questions about China – like why most

00:18:31.270 --> 00:18:36.210
of the rare earth metals necessary to build
electronics come from one rural town in Inner

00:18:36.210 --> 00:18:42.470
Mongolia, and why Chinese censorship is both
